<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

namespace {{namespace}};

use Nyholm\Psr7\Response;
use Psr\Http\Message\ResponseInterface;
use Psr\Http\Message\ServerRequestInterface;
use Psr\Http\Server\MiddlewareInterface;
use Psr\Http\Server\RequestHandlerInterface;

/**
 * The write routes of {{entityClass}} refuse a caller nobody recognised.
 *
 * Reads stay anonymous — a blog nobody can read is not a blog — and `POST`, `PUT` and `DELETE`
 * are declared behind this. It is generated INTO your plugin rather than imported from the
 * framework so you own it: widen it, narrow it, or replace it with your own policy.
 *
 * ── WHY THIS EXISTS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Measured on a fresh app, served for real: `POST /{{table}}` with no key, no session and no CSRF
 * token answered **201 CREATED** and the row persisted (greenhouse `evidence/0991`). The scaffold
 * was publishing three mutating routes with `middleware: []`, so nothing judged who was calling.
 *
 * The skeleton already carries the rule for the other surface — «ninguna operación protegida se
 * publica sin quién la proteja», checked at boot in `OperationsHttpPlugin::assertGuarded()`, whose
 * own words are the reason this file exists: **a gate that is present on one surface and absent on
 * the other stands at the height of the lower one.** That guard governs operations exposed through
 * `config/http.php`; a scaffold publishes ROUTES, so it never saw them.
 *
 * ── WHAT IT DOES NOT DO ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * It does not decide WHO may write, only that somebody was recognised. What that somebody is
 * allowed to do belongs to a policy, and a policy can only require what an operation declares.
 *
 * It reads the request ATTRIBUTE and never a class, which is what keeps this plugin from depending
 * on `milpa/auth`: whoever puts `milpa.auth` there declares its shape — an object with an `actor`
 * carrying an `id`. The same seam the framework's own HTTP projector reads. With `milpa/auth`
 * wired (`config/boot.php` does it in one line, opt-in) these routes open; without it they refuse,
 * which is the safe direction for a route that writes.
 */
final class {{class}} implements MiddlewareInterface
{
    public function process(ServerRequestInterface $request, RequestHandlerInterface $handler): ResponseInterface
    {
        if ($this->actor($request) !== null) {
            return $handler->handle($request);
        }

        return new Response(
            401,
            ['Content-Type' => 'application/json'],
            (string) json_encode([
                'error' => 'this write needs a recognised caller',
                // The refusal names the fix, because one that does not is one the reader cannot act
                // on. Both halves: how to open it, and where to remove the gate if that is wanted.
                'how' => 'Wire identity so a caller can be recognised — `php bin/coa capabilities:enable '
                    . 'milpa/auth` and the `milpa.auth` request attribute reaches this middleware. To publish '
                    . 'these writes to anyone instead, drop {{class}}::class from the write routes in '
                    . '{{pluginClass}}::routes() — deliberately, not by forgetting.',
            ], JSON_UNESCAPED_UNICODE | JSON_UNESCAPED_SLASHES),
        );
    }

    /**
     * The recognised caller's id, or null when nobody was recognised.
     *
     * Read defensively on purpose: this middleware travels in YOUR app and has to survive an
     * attribute put there by a version of the identity chain it was not generated against. An
     * attribute of an unexpected shape means «nobody was recognised» — never «everybody is».
     */
    private function actor(ServerRequestInterface $request): ?string
    {
        $auth = $request->getAttribute('milpa.auth');
        if (! \is_object($auth) || ! property_exists($auth, 'actor')) {
            return null;
        }

        $actor = $auth->actor;
        if (! \is_object($actor) || ! property_exists($actor, 'id') || ! \is_string($actor->id)) {
            return null;
        }

        return trim($actor->id) === '' ? null : $actor->id;
    }
}
